/T (You may realize, as you engage with this thinking routine, that the system you have selected is more complicated or abstract than you originally thought. This is okay. Help students look closely at the parts that they are interested in, and feel free to help them seek more information by asking each other, by using books or the internet, or by consulting with community members.It is okay if students have unanswered questions about the system. Consider documenting these questions to revisit if the opportunity presents itself later on. Note: This thinking routine is adapted from the Agency by Design Parts, People, Interactions thinking routine.)
/E (You may realize, as you engage with this thinking routine, that the system you have selected is more complicated or abstract than you originally thought. This is okay. Help students look closely at the parts that they are interested in, and feel free to help them seek more information by asking each other, by using books or the internet, or by consulting with community members.It is okay if students have unanswered questions about the system. Consider documenting these questions to revisit if the opportunity presents itself later on. Note: This thinking routine is adapted from the Agency by Design Parts, People, Interactions thinking routine.)
