3023 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1453 0 R
/T (For the perspectives step, encourage students to imagine different physical viewpoints if appropriate. Encourage them to think broadly about how different people interact with or are connected to the object or system including considering who is involved in making it, who is affected by it, and who cares about it. When possible, encourage students to gather information about other perspectives, for example by interviewing people or doing research. For the your involvement step, encourage students to consider the different ways the object or system influences their lives or the lives of other people. Encourage them to consider any feelings, assumptions, connections, beliefs, attitudes, or associations they have with it. As with the other routines that begin with the naming of parts, students can make their thinking visible by creating lists, sketches, and diagrams.)
/E (For the perspectives step, encourage students to imagine different physical viewpoints if appropriate. Encourage them to think broadly about how different people interact with or are connected to the object or system including considering who is involved in making it, who is affected by it, and who cares about it. When possible, encourage students to gather information about other perspectives, for example by interviewing people or doing research. For the your involvement step, encourage students to consider the different ways the object or system influences their lives or the lives of other people. Encourage them to consider any feelings, assumptions, connections, beliefs, attitudes, or associations they have with it. As with the other routines that begin with the naming of parts, students can make their thinking visible by creating lists, sketches, and diagrams.)
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
