3155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 8696 0 R
/T (This routine provides an opportunity for children to make their thinking visible. Children can showtheir thinking through drawings, photos, role play, and/or writing.)
/E (This routine provides an opportunity for children to make their thinking visible. Children can showtheir thinking through drawings, photos, role play, and/or writing.)
/Pg 89 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
3156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 8697 0 R
/T (If you are using this thinking routine with young learners, consider having them explore an objector a system they have direct experience with. This way, all children can apply and build upontheir prior knowledge in meaningful ways. We suggest you start with objects or systems in yourclassroom or community that students use regularly.)
/E (If you are using this thinking routine with young learners, consider having them explore an objector a system they have direct experience with. This way, all children can apply and build upontheir prior knowledge in meaningful ways. We suggest you start with objects or systems in yourclassroom or community that students use regularly.)
/Pg 89 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
3157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 8698 0 R
/T (Help children develop their language and literacy skills by encouraging them to work togetherand to discuss what they notice with a partner. Consider creating a class word wall of descriptivelanguage that the class can use and add to over the course of the school year.)
/E (Help children develop their language and literacy skills by encouraging them to work togetherand to discuss what they notice with a partner. Consider creating a class word wall of descriptivelanguage that the class can use and add to over the course of the school year.)
/Pg 89 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
3158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 8699 0 R
/T (To encourage children to deepen their understanding of the parts, purposes, and complexities ofthe object under study, have them take apart the object. Then, repeat the thinking routine whilelooking closely at the separate parts.)
/E (To encourage children to deepen their understanding of the parts, purposes, and complexities ofthe object under study, have them take apart the object. Then, repeat the thinking routine whilelooking closely at the separate parts.)
/Pg 89 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
3159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 8700 0 R
/T (You may realize, as you engage with this thinking routine, that the system you have selected ismore complicated or abstract than you originally thought. This is okay. Help students look closelyat the parts that they are interested in, and feel free to help them seek more information by askingeach other, by using books or the internet, or by consulting with community members. It is okayif students have unanswered questions about the system. Consider documenting these questionsto revisit if the opportunity presents itself later on.)
